# Youth Timer semantic test — runs against the real headless runtime.
#
# Gate C-2 wires Start/Pause/Resume/Cancel/Reset to real host schedules
# through context.time(). This test exercises the synchronous half of
# that contract: every command below completes inside one ordinary turn,
# so its outcome is directly observable. Autonomous ScheduleElapsed
# delivery is not exercised here -- `.youth-test` has no way to wait on
# real elapsed time or fire a virtual-clock wake, only Youth's own
# workspace test suite can drive that deterministically (see FINDINGS.md,
# the TIMER-F013 note on injection testing).
#
# NOTE on `activate <name>` vs `key <k>`: `activate` is direct semantic
# injection and bypasses host interaction policy (enabled-button
# filtering, focus, shortcut resolution); `key` passes through it. Every
# command below that matters for host policy is exercised at least once
# via `key`, not only `activate`. See ../FINDINGS.md, TIMER-F010.

mount
expect text countdown "00:00"
expect text mode "Mode: idle"
expect text sessions "Sessions: 0"
expect text notice ""
expect focus none

# Configure a session.
activate add-1m
expect text mode "Mode: idle"

# Start via its keyboard shortcut (Enter): proves the declared
# Shortcut::Enter reaches the guest as the same "start" command, and that
# it actually creates a real host schedule -- if schedule_after had
# failed, this command would return an error and the test would fail
# here rather than silently continuing.
key enter
expect text mode "Mode: running"

# The countdown node retargeted from literal text to a real host-owned
# schedule reference (TIMER-F004) -- not a value this app resolved or
# formatted itself; `expect countdown` cannot read what it will display,
# only that the host is now the one who will.
expect countdown countdown

# Configuration is fixed once running.
activate add-1m
expect text mode "Mode: running"

# Pause via its shortcut, proving the host returned a usable paused
# schedule (a stale-generation error from a broken pause implementation
# would surface as a rejected command here, not a silent no-op). The
# countdown stays bound to a live schedule -- the *new*, paused-generation
# identity the host just returned, per apply_command's contract.
key "p"
expect text mode "Mode: paused"
expect countdown countdown

# Resume via its shortcut, retargeting again to the resumed generation.
key "u"
expect text mode "Mode: running"
expect countdown countdown

# Durable state survives a restart: mode is the canonical model, not a
# derived display string, and the active schedule identity persists with
# it (the runtime revalidates it as part of loading, not just formats it).
# The countdown is reconstructed as a live schedule reference, not
# reformatted as a literal value.
restart
expect text mode "Mode: running"
expect text sessions "Sessions: 0"
expect countdown countdown

# Cancel via its shortcut retires the host schedule and returns to idle --
# and the display retargets back to a literal, configured-duration string.
key escape
expect text mode "Mode: idle"
expect text countdown "01:00"

# Start again, then Reset via its shortcut -- valid even though nothing
# elapsed, and also retires the newly created schedule, retargeting the
# countdown back to literal text.
key enter
expect text mode "Mode: running"
expect countdown countdown
key backspace
expect text mode "Mode: idle"
expect text sessions "Sessions: 0"
expect text countdown "01:00"

# A freshly created record needed no migration, so no recovery notice.
expect text notice ""
